@*
    Circuit-independent comment box.

    The input is intentionally UNBOUND. A `@bind` would make Blazor the owner of the
    element's value, and offline-actions.js clears the field itself after enqueuing — with
    the circuit down the server copy would drift, and on the next re-render Blazor would
    push the stale text back into a field the user has already seen emptied. Leaving it
    uncontrolled means the DOM is the single owner of the draft text.

    The submit button carries no @onclick for the same single-writer reason as the attempt
    buttons, and is never `disabled`: a disabled button is skipped by the delegated
    handler, and the flag driving it could not be updated offline anyway. Empty input is
    rejected by the handler itself (it enqueues nothing), and the greyed-out look comes
    from a pure-CSS `:placeholder-shown` sibling rule that needs no circuit.
*@

<div class="comment-form">
    <input id="@InputId" placeholder="Write a comment..." class="comment-input" maxlength="1024" />
    <button type="button" class="comment-submit"
            data-bw-action="comment" data-bw-boulder="@BoulderId" data-bw-text="#@InputId">
        Post
    </button>
</div>

@code {
    [Parameter] public Guid BoulderId { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The id <c>data-bw-text</c> resolves the draft text from.</summary>
    [Parameter] public string InputId { get; set; } = "bw-comment-input";
}
